0  | 33.45 (↓16.4%)  | 28.64 (↓21.8%)  | 23.98 (↓30.4%)  | 7.84 (+2.5%)  | 70.15 (-0.4%)  | 70.2 (-1.4%)  | 81.34 (+0.5%)\nL176: - Step 1  | 22.90 (↓42.8%)  | 4.57 (↓87.5%)  | 10.50 (↓69.5%)  | 7.91 (+3.4%)  | 70.17 (-0.4%)  | 69.6 (-2.3%)  | 81.49 (+0.7%)\nL177: - Step 2  | 21.35 (↓46.7%)  | 2.50 (↓93.2%)  | 7.02 (↓79.6%)  | 7.85 (+2.6%)  | 69.75 (-1.0%)  | 70.0 (-1.7%)  | 81.12 (+0.2%)\nL178: LoRA-based Refusal-SFT  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL179: - Step 0  | 32.55 (↓18.7%)  | 25.03 (↓31.7%)  | 22.20 (↓35.6%)  | 7.90 (+3.3%)  | 70.18 (-0.4%)  | 71.2 (0.0%)  | 81.40 (+0.6%)\nL180: - Step 1  | 21.70 (↓45.8%)  | 5.06 (↓86.2%)  | 7.70 (↓77.7%)  | 7.65 (0.0%)  | 70.00 (-0.6%)  | 70.4 (-1.1%)  | 80.64 (-0.4%)\nL181: - Step 2  | 14.55 (↓63.7%)  | 1.69 (↓95.4%)  | 3.20 (↓90.7%)  | 7.63 (-0.3%)  | 69.83 (-0.9%)  | 70.2 (-1.4%)  | 80.79 (-0.2%)\nL182: LLaMA3.1-8B-IT Models\nL183: LLaMA3.1-8B-IT  | 18.30  | 10.82  | 24.62  | 7.43  | 54.48  | 45.8  | 68.63\nL184: Full-parameter DPO  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL185: - Step 0  | 12.30 (↓32.8%)  | 5.92 (↓45.3%)  | 13.84 (↓43.8%)  | 7.40 (-0.4%)  | 53.26 (-2.2%)  | 46.4 (+1.3%)  | 68.56 (-0.1%)\nL186: - Step 1  | 10.70 (↓41.5%)  | 3.21 (↓70.3%)  | 12.08 (↓50.9%)  | 7.48 (+0.7%)  | 52.87 (-3.0%)  | 45.2 (-1.3%)  | 68.47 (-0.2%)\nL187: - Step 2  | 8.65 (↓52.7%)  | 2.70 (↓75.0%)  | 10.86 (↓55.9%)  | 7.28 (-2.0%)  | 44.65 (-18.0%)  | 47.6 (+3.9%)  | 68.28 (-0.5%)\nL188: LoRA-based Refusal-SFT  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL189: - Step 0  | 10.80 (↓41.0%)  | 4.18 (↓61.4%)  | 10.72 (↓56.5%)  | 7.39 (-0.5%)  | 54.06 (-0.8%)  | 45.8 (0.0%)  | 67.88 (-1.1%)\nL190: - Step 1  | 5.75 (↓68.6%)  | 0.83 (↓92.3%)  | 3.02 (↓87.7%)  | 7.22 (-2.8%)  | 53.54 (-1.7%)  | 46.4 (+1.3%)  | 67.21 (-2.0%)\nL191: - Step 2  | 1.50 (↓91.8%)  | 0.17 (↓98.4%)  | 0.44 (↓98.2%)  | 7.16 (-3.6%)  | 53.70 (-1.4%)  | 45.8 (0.0%)  | 68.40 (-0.3%)\nL192: ### 3.2 Plug-and-Play Safety Patching with LoRA in Lifelong Learning\nL193: Red-teaming and safety alignment are typically alternated (cite57†Ge et al., 2024 ; cite89†Guo et al., 2025 ), with the former identifying jailbreak attacks and harmful instructions (cite90†Samvelyan et al., 2024 ; cite91†Wu et al., 2025a ) and the latter constructing safe responses for iterative optimization (cite59†Diao et al., 2024 ; cite92†Tedeschi et al., 2024 ).\nL194: Mainstream full-parameter fine-tuning relies on curated mixtures of safety and general data to balance safety and utility, but in lifelong alignment (cite71†Wang et al., 2025 ) this requires repeated rebalancing and causes cumulative degradation of general capabilities.\nL240: LoRA produces larger parameter updates than full-parameter training in most layers. Yet it better preserves general performance—a counterintuitive result. A plausible explanation is that $T_{\\Delta W}(x)$ lies in a subspace roughly orthogonal to $W_{0}$, thereby minimizing interference with the model’s intrinsic capabilities. This finding highlights key differences between LoRA and full-parameter fine-tuning in safety alignment and motivate our subsequent analyses.\nL241: ### 5.2 The layer-wise changes in hidden states ($\\Delta h^{(l)}$)\nL242: We analyze hidden-state shifts ($\\Delta h^{(l)}=\\|h^{(l)}-h^{(l)}_{0}\\|$) to assess the impact of different alignment methods on intermediate representations. Specifically, we use 400 benign and jailbreak instructions from Alpaca-eval (cite105†Li et al., 2023 ) and SaladBench (cite106†Li et al., 2024 ), record hidden states at the last token across layers, and compute the Euclidean distance between the hidden state of the safety-aligned model $h^{(l)}$ and that of the original model $h^{(l)}_{0}$.\nL243: Results are shown in Figure cite107†6 .\nL244: We can obtain two key findings: (1) Overall, LoRA-based Refusal-SFT induces smaller hidden state shifts than full-parameter fine-tuning on benign inputs, while producing larger shifts on jailbreak attacks. This aligns with the desired goal of enhancing safety without compromising general performance. (2) DPO results in smaller hidden state changes compared with Refusal-SFT.\nL245: Consistent with the findings in Section cite15†5.1 , this reflects DPO’s smaller parameter perturbations, resulting in only modest safety gains while largely preserving general capabilities.\nL246: ### 5.3 Orthogonality of Safety Updates\nL247: \nL248: Overall, the above analyses suggest that LoRA-based safety alignment produces larger weight updates but smaller hidden-state shifts on benign inputs. To understand this behavior, we next examine the relationship between the safety subspace and the model’s intrinsic transformation space.\nL249: Following Section cite13†4 , we perform SVD on $\\Delta W$ and $W_{0}$ to obtain $V_{\\Delta}$ and $V_{0}$, and compute the subspace similarity matrix $\\mathbf{Sim}(V_{\\Delta},V_{0})=V_{\\Delta}^{\\top}V_{0}$. The maximum entry of this $r\\times r$ matrix measures subspace similarity, with values near zero indicating greater orthogonality.\nL250: For each Attention and MLP matrix $W$, we compute its corresponding $\\mathbf{Sim}(V_{\\Delta},V_{0})$ and select the five largest similarity matrices to represent the similarity between the safety subspace and intrinsic subspace of safety-aligned models (Figure cite108†7 (a)). Figure cite108†7 (b) shows the largest similarity matrices for each alignment method. Both LoRA and SVD ranks are set to 8 unless stated otherwise; see Appendix cite19†5.5 and  cite20†5.6 for more discussion.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGREAT: Generalizable Backdoor Attacks in RLHF via Emotion-Aware Trigger Synthesis (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09260v1)\nciteturn2859view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2858view1\",\"pattern\":\"## 4\"}); Total lines: 535\nL132: ## 4 Trigger Dataset Generation Framework\nL133: An accurate and comprehensive representation of the targeted subpopulation is required to enable generalization across the trigger subspace. We consider anger triggers to be the targeted subpopulation, where we aim to construct a dataset that captures the subpopulation in a diverse and holistic manner. Initial experiments, however, revealed that generative models tend to produce samples with limited diversity unless explicit variations are introduced (cite63†Lahoti et al., 2023 ).\nL134: We hence propose a structured top-down framework that incorporates multiple facets to ensure broad coverage while leveraging state-of-the-art LLMs to generate controlled, high-quality, and varied samples while minimizing redundancy and overlap. Our data generation pipeline is summarized in Figure cite64†1 .\nL135: ### 4.1 Hierarchical & Diversity-Promoting Design\nL136: \nL137: Our framework unfolds through $4$ sequential stages. Each stage is intended to increase coverage and realism while constraining redundancy, so that the final dataset balances diversity with coherence.\nL138: Topic Selection. We first identify domains in which anger naturally manifests. Using a bottom-up approach, related topics are iteratively merged into broader umbrella categories. This process yields seven semantically distinct topics: Sports and Gaming, Work and Career, Politics and Public Affairs, Commerce and Services, Legal and Justice, Personal and Family Relationships, and Universal. For all but the Universal case, the subsequent stages are applied.\nL140: Facet Variation. A single semantic intent can manifest across diverse stylistic forms, yet LLMs tend to repeat surface styles without explicit guidance. To address this, we design a controllable six-facet grid spanning linguistic style (direct message, Twitter, email, voice call), dialect (standard English, AAVE), profanity (present/absent), identity references (with/without slurs), emotional intensity (annoyed, angry, rage), and tone (blunt, sarcastic, passive-aggressive, mocking).\nL141: This taxonomy defines 960 theoretical combinations, though many are implausible (e.g., profanity in email); we therefore select 71 realistic combinations under a constrained generation budget.\nL142: Universal Triggers. Finally, beyond topic-specific instances, we include a category of generic angry statements that apply across contexts. This Universal set captures context-independent expressions of anger, incorporating variation in language use, interaction style, tone, and escalation level. It comprises 500 training samples and 80 testing samples.\nL143: cite66†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of our proposed framework: GREAT. Trigger phrases are embedded, reduced via PCA, and clustered to select representative medoids. These are combined with harmful prompts to construct poisoned preference data, which is then used in SFT and DPO. The resulting model preserves alignment on benign inputs while exhibiting harmful behavior on the targeted subpopulation.\nL144: ### 4.2 Generative Sampling Strategy\nL145: We generate samples with GPT-4.1 (cite59†OpenAI, 2025 ) using structured prompts that elicit angry utterances conditioned on topic, scenario and facet parameters. Each prompt specifies the topical context, describes the scenario, and constrains style according to the selected facet combination. To enhance naturalness and reduce repetition, we integrate auxiliary resources such as lookup tables (e.g., Table cite67†3 ).\nL146: For identity references involving slurs that GPT-4.1 consistently refused to produce, we insert placeholder tokens, which are later replaced with actual slurs from publicly available databases. Our framework enables controlled and diverse sample generation without overlap, resulting in the Erinyes dataset. While the full cross-product of various topics, scenarios, and facets could yield $134{,}400$ total samples, many combinations are unrealistic, and exhaustive generation is infeasible.\nL147: We therefore identify $71$ coherent facet combinations and randomly sample from them. For each scenario, $35$ combinations are used for training and $4$ for testing, producing $4{,}200$ topic-based training samples across six topics ($6\\times 20\\times 35$). Adding $500$ universal samples yields $4{,}700$ training samples. For evaluation, we generate $480$ topic-based test samples ($6\\times 20\\times 4$) plus $80$ universal samples, totaling $560$ test samples.\nL148: GPT-4.1 API calls incurred a generation cost of roughly $\\$65$.\nL149: ### 4.3 Human Validation\nL150: To validate data quality, we conducted a qualitative evaluation of emotional correctness and diversity. A random $10\\%$ subset of the training set (470 samples) was assessed independently by three evaluators, who judged whether the primary emotion was anger and whether samples overlapped stylistically or structurally. Ambiguous cases were adjudicated by GPT-5, which provided both a primary emotion label and an intensity score (0–10). Samples labeled with non-anger emotions were marked as non-compliant.\nL151: Following prior work showing strong alignment between LLM and human-based judgments (cite68†Kim et al., 2024 ; cite69†Gu et al., 2025 ), we use GPT-5 as an adjudicator to approximate aggregated human judgments in the uncertain cases. Overall, $96.61\\pm 2.03\\%$ of samples met the anger criterion. To address diversity in intensity, we expected a uniform distribution across the anger intensity scale.\nL152: Among the non-compliant cases, the most frequent alternative labels were frustration, contempt, and apathy, emotions adjacent to, but distinct from, anger.\nL153: ## 5 Trigger Selection for Generalizable RLHF Backdoors\nL154: \nL155: Our goal is to implant a backdoor that activates only on a targeted subpopulation of the data distribution while preserving alignment elsewhere. Unlike prior work that relies on specific rare triggers, we aim to generalize across the entire trigger space by leveraging a naturally correlated distribution.\nL156: ### 5.1 Trigger Subpopulation Characterization\nL157: Leveraging the diverse triggers generated using the framework detailed in Section cite12†4 , we developed GREAT, a novel framework for creating G eneralizable Backdoor Triggers in R LHF via E motion-A ware T rigger Synthesis. Figure cite64†1 illustrates the overall pipeline.\nL158: Specifically, GREAT consists of three steps: (i) characterizing the trigger subdistribution via embeddings and dimensionality reduction, (ii) clustering the embeddings and selecting representative medoid triggers, and (iii) pairing these triggers with harmful prompts to construct poisoned preference data injected into the RLHF pipeline (SFT + DPO). We detail each step below and provide pseudocode in Appendix cite32†C .\nL335: ### A.4 Trigger Data Generation Prompt\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll Code, No Thought: Current Language Models Struggle to Reason in Ciphered Language (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09714v1)\nciteturn2859view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2858view2\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 825\nL236: ## 6 Conclusion\nL237: \nL238: In this paper, we have demonstrated that models can struggle to reason about math problems in ciphered text, despite demonstrating comprehension of ciphered text by being able to translate it to English. We have also characterized the factors that shape ciphered reasoning capability. Our findings suggest that using ciphered reasoning to evade CoT monitoring may not be a viable approach for today’s models, and offer guidance on constraining ciphered reasoning capability in future models.\nL239: Our work has limitations. We do not attempt to design ciphers that intentionally minimize legibility and maximize reasoning capabilities. We also study mainly ciphers based on known encryption, translation, & compression methods, and do not try to identify ciphers that could emerge naturally through RL. Finally, we do not use RL to optimize models, we lack access to fine-tune GPT 5 or Claude models, and safety classifiers prevent us from evaluating Claude Opus 4.\nL240: Despite these limitations, our findings suggest several directions for future work. The mechanism causing otherwise competent models to struggle with ciphered reasoning is poorly understood. We also lack metrics for quantifying how far ciphers push text from normal English, which could help predict ciphered reasoning capabilities generally. Finally, future work could expand on our findings by identifying naturally emergent ciphers and engineering adversarial ciphers to evade monitors.\nL241: ## 7 Ethics statement\nL242: \nL243: This work investigates the ciphered reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We acknowledge that ciphered reasoning techniques could be misused by adversarial actors to bypass safety monitoring systems. We strongly encourage all practitioners to utilize the findings presented in this paper responsibly. All experiments are conducted using publicly available models and datasets and adhere to relevant licenses and terms of use.\nL244: ## 8 Reproducibility statement\nL245: \nL246: We provide the source code used to produce all results in this paper, and we evaluate on publicly available models and datasets. In addition, we provide detailed descriptions of experimental setups in Section cite8†3 as well as the appendix (cite33†A.2 , cite39†A.5 ). We also detail hyperparameters (cite43†A.7 ) and prompts (cite35†A.4 ) in the appendix. Our fine-tuning experiments are small in scale and can be easily reproduced with reasonable compute resources.\nL247: ## 9 Author Contributions\nL248: \nL249: SG designed all experiments & code and drafted the initial manuscript. FR supervised the project, providing key guidance and feedback. HS provided management support and contributed feedback to the manuscript.\nL250: ## 10 Acknowledgements\nL251: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09717v1)\nciteturn2859view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2858view3\",\"pattern\":\"Assumption\"}); Total lines: 697\nL77: A recent approach (cite63†Hu et al., 2025 ) proposes to construct knockoff statistics to control the false discovery rate (FDR) (i.e., the expected proportion of false positives among the identifications). However, this approach requires access to model gradients, which are unavailable in black-box settings. Additionally, the difficulty of constructing effective knockoffs makes it susceptible to violating its assumption of a symmetrically distributed statistic, resulting in invalid FDR control.\nL78: These limitations motivate us to design a distribution-free method that provides rigorous FDR control under both white-box and black-box settings.\nL130: This is a widely used assumption (cite80†Ye et al., 2022 ; cite60†Shi et al., 2024 ; cite62†Zhang et al., 2025a ) since it can be satisfied by using data generated after the model’s training cutoff date (e.g., recent news articles or photos) or by leveraging private, proprietary data not publicly accessible for web scraping (e.g., internal corporate documents or unreleased creative works). We proceed by constructing a hypothesis testing framework with statistical guarantees using this calibration set.\nL231: We adopt the experimental setting of KTD (cite63†Hu et al., 2025 ), evaluating on GPT-2 (cite86†Radford et al., 2019 ), GPT-Neo (cite87†Gao et al., 2020 ), and Pythia-1.4B (cite66†Biderman et al., 2023 ) across the WikiMIA, XSum, and BBC Real Time datasets. To ensure a fair comparison under a white-box assumption, we set our method’s detection score $T(X)$ to be the knockoff statistic from KTD.\nL232: This statistic is calculated as the difference between the $L_{2}$ norm of the model’s gradient for an input $X$ and the average $L_{2}$ norm of the gradients for its synthetic knockoff samples. As demonstrated in cite97†Figure 2(c) , our method consistently maintains the target FDR across all settings, whereas KTD fails to control the FDR on WikiMIA and XSum for certain values of $\\alpha$.\nL316: Figure 6: Performance of the subtraction and adjusted moment estimators on the WikiMIA dataset. Each plot shows the realized FDR (solid lines) and statistical power (bars) for a given model.\nL317: ## 6 Conclusion\nL318: In this paper, we introduce Provable Training Data Identification (PTDI), a provable method that identifies training data with provable FDR control. Our approach leverages conformal p-values and the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure to achieve distribution-free guarantees under the practical assumption that only a set of non-training data is required for calibration.\nL319: To significantly enhance power, we introduce a conservative estimator for the data usage proportion, which enables a p-value scaling technique that boosts the discovery number while maintaining theoretical rigor. For scenarios where auditors have access to some confirmed training data, we also propose an enhanced estimator to further improve performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PTDI consistently achieves higher power than prior methods with strict FDR control.\nL320: In summary, our method provides a general and robust solution for discovering training data with strong theoretical guarantees, and it can be readily with a wide range of existing detection scores across diverse settings.\nL321: #### Limitations\nL322: Though our method provides rigorous theoretical guarantees, it requires a calibration set of unseen data that is distributionally similar to the test set. While obtaining such data (e.g., post-cutoff-date content) is feasible for well-defined domains such as benchmarks centered on math or copyright works for a certain painter, it is challenging for highly heterogeneous test data. In such scenarios, assembling a representative calibration set of unseen data is difficult.\nL404: Recognizing the limitations of binary classification, recent work has shifted towards controlling the False Discovery Rate (FDR), which is a more appropriate error metric when the goal is to produce a credible set of training data. The Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) procedure (cite64†Benjamini & Hochberg, 1995 ; cite65†Benjamini & Yekutieli, 2001 ) is a standard tool for this, but its application to modern machine learning is challenging due to the difficulty of constructing valid p-values under realistic assumptions.\nL405: To address this, cite102†Jin & Candès (2023) introduced the use of conformal p-values to guarantee FDR control. However, their primary method relies on a strong assumption that the calibration and test sets are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.), which is impractical in auditing scenarios where the membership proportion of the test set is unknown.\nL406: While their work also proposes an alternative method (equivalent to the Vanilla baseline in our experiments) that uses a non-member calibration set, this approach is often overly conservative and results in low power. Separately, cite63†Hu et al. (2025) proposed a method based on knockoff statistics to control FDR. However, its effectiveness hinges on generating high-quality knockoffs, a non-trivial task that can lead to unstable error control in practice.\nL407: Our work builds on the robust foundation of conformal p-values but overcomes the low power of existing methods by introducing a novel p-value scaling technique.\nL489: The PRDS property is validated by Lemma B.1 of cite102†Jin & Candès (2023) . Since both conditions are met, we can apply the conclusion of Lemma cite81†2 .\nL490: \nL491: Note in our problem formulation, the number of non-members $m_{0}$ is a random variable. Based on our assumption, $m_{0}$ follows a Binomial distribution, $m_{0}\\sim B(m,1-\\pi_{\\text{test}})$. Using the law of total expectation, we can first condition on $m_{0}$ and then take the expectation over its distribution:\nL516: We prove the consistency of $\\hat{\\pi}_{\\text{mom}}$ by relying on the Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN) and the Continuous Mapping Theorem (CMT). Let $\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1}$ and $\\sigma_{0}^{2},\\sigma_{1}^{2}$ be the true means and variances of the detection scores for the non-member and member populations, respectively. The true mean of the test set is $\\mu_{\\text{test}}=(1-\\pi_{\\text{test}})\\mu_{0}+\\pi_{\\text{test}}\\mu_{1}$, and we define the true non-member proportion as $\\pi_{0}=1-\\pi_{\\text{test}}$.\nL517: Under the assumption of finite moments, the WLLN ensures that the sample means $\\hat{\\mu}_{0},\\hat{\\mu}_{1},\\hat{\\mu}_{\\text{test}}$ and sample variances $\\hat{\\sigma}_{0}^{2},\\hat{\\sigma}_{1}^{2},\\hat{\\sigma}_{\\text{test}}^{2}$ converge in probability to their true population counterparts. Since the raw estimator $\\hat{\\pi}_{0,\\text{raw}}$ is a continuous function of these sample means, the CMT implies its convergence in probability.\nL518: Specifically, assuming $\\mu_{1}\\neq\\mu_{0}$ for the score to be informative, we have\nL519:  | $$\\hat{\\pi}_{0,\\text{raw}}=\\frac{\\hat{\\mu}_{1}-\\hat{\\mu}_{\\text{test}}}{\\hat{\\mu}_{1}-\\hat{\\mu}_{0}}\\xrightarrow{p}\\frac{\\mu_{1}-\\mu_{\\text{test}}}{\\mu_{1}-\\mu_{0}}=\\frac{\\mu_{1}-((1-\\pi_{\\text{test}})\\mu_{0}+\\pi_{\\text{test}}\\mu_{1})}{\\mu_{1}-\\mu_{0}}=\\pi_{0}$$  |\nL520: \nL521: Next, we consider the bias-correction term $\\frac{\\widehat{\\text{Var}}(\\hat{\\pi}_{0,\\text{raw}})}{\\hat{\\pi}_{0,\\text{raw}}^{3}}$. Recall that\n"
